<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!-- Empty on purpose, and this file is worth reading before anyone "fixes" it. The Mac
     app fetches patents over the network and reads PDFs the reader drops on it, and
     both of those are exactly the shape of thing that makes an engineer reach for the
     App Sandbox checkbox. That would be a mistake here, for four reasons.

     1. **App Sandbox is off, as it is for ShakespeareReader, and for the same reason.**
        `AnswerService.downloader` takes swift-huggingface's default on macOS,
        `~/.cache/huggingface/hub`, shared with every other MLX tool and with Python's
        `huggingface_hub`. Sandboxing redirects that into `~/Library/Containers/`, so a
        first launch re-downloads weights already sitting on disk. That cost is *larger*
        here than next door: PatentReader loads Qwen3-4B (2.2 GB) and
        nomic-embed-text-v1.5 (~550 MB), and with the sandbox off it shares the first
        with ShakespeareReader and the second with `Tools/embedder-tool`, so a machine
        that has run either pays for neither.

     2. **Network access therefore needs no entitlement.**
        `com.apple.security.network.client` exists to *re-permit* what the sandbox takes
        away; with no sandbox there is nothing to re-permit. Adding it would be cargo
        cult — it is inert outside a sandbox — and would suggest a containment this
        target does not have.

     3. **Nor do user-selected file reads.** `com.apple.security.files.user-selected.
        read-only` is likewise a sandbox exception. A dropped PDF and a `.fileImporter`
        URL are both ordinary file reads here.

     4. **Hardened runtime is on and needs no exception.** It restricts code injection,
        library loading, JIT and debugger attachment, never filesystem or network. MLX
        compiles Metal libraries at runtime through `MTLDevice.makeLibrary(source:)` and
        the out-of-process compiler, so there are no `MAP_JIT` pages and no
        `com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit`. Debug builds still get `get-task-allow`
        merged in by `CODE_SIGN_INJECT_BASE_ENTITLEMENTS`, so lldb attaches.

     The counter-argument, stated fairly rather than waved away: an app that fetches
     arbitrary URLs and parses somebody else's HTML is a good candidate for a sandbox,
     and if this ever shipped outside an examples repo it should have one. The trade is
     explicit — a container costs a 2.75 GB re-download on first launch — and this file
     is where that trade is recorded rather than rediscovered. -->
<dict/>
</plist>
